body:
Alright, this is an awkward one. Let me set it up.

``` php
// Plugin: Devices
class DevicesTable extends Table {

    public function addDevice($Device, array $options = []) {

        $options += ['associated' => ['DeviceInformations']];
        return $this->save($Device, $options);

    }

    public static function defaultConnectionName() {

        return 'devices';

    }

    public function initialize(array $config) {

        parent::initialize($config);
        $this->hasOne('Devices.DeviceInformations');

    }

}
```

``` php
// Plugin: Network
class RackTable extends Table {

    public function addRack($Rack, array $options = []) {

        $options += [
            'associated' => ['Devices.DeviceInformations'],
            'atomic' => false
        ];
        $createRack = function () use ($Rack, $options) {
            // Scenario 1: Can't save device
            $Device = $Rack->get('device');
            $deviceOptions = [
                'associated' => ['DeviceInformations']
                'atomic' => false
            ];
            $added = $this->Devices->addDevice($Device, $deviceOptions); // Returns false

            // Scenario 2: Saves Rack and Device but not DeviceInformations
            return $this->save($Rack, $options);
        };
        return $this->connection()->transactional($createRack);

    }

    public static function defaultConnectionName() {

        return 'network';

    }

    public function initialize(array $config) {

        parent::initialize($config);
        $this->belongsTo('Devices.Devices');

    }

}
```

In both scenarios I get a simple `false` in response. After some debugging I managed to track down the problem. In the `addRack()` method if I set `$options['validate'] = false;` everything is saved exactly as expected in any scenario that I use. So then I modified the method like this:

``` php
public function addRack($Rack, array $options = []) {

    $options += ['atomic' => false];
    $createRack = function () use ($Rack, $options) {
        $Device = $Rack->get('device');
        $deviceOptions = [
            'associated' => ['DeviceInformations']
            'atomic' => false
        ];
        $validates = $this->Devices->validate($Device, $deviceOptions);
        debug($validates);
        return false;
    };
    return $this->connection()->transactional($createRack);

}
```

... and that's when I found it. The problem was coming from the [`EntityValidator`](https://github.com/cakephp/cakephp/blob/3.0/src/ORM/EntityValidator.php#L49-L71) class. On the `DevicesTable` when validation was being called the `$assoc` key ends up `null` which means that the `$map` variable is never set so I get an error for the variable not set and an error for an invalid type on the [foreach loop](https://github.com/cakephp/cakephp/blob/3.0/src/ORM/EntityValidator.php#L89).

Something was causing that `$this->_table->association($key);` call to return `null` and the only way I could find that out was to `return false;` to let the php error display. If I ran the save functionality outside of a transaction it would fail every time by saying that the `network.devices` table did not exist on the connection.

I hope this makes sense because I've been able to get this error consistently. I'm using the most current version of 3.0 which leads me to believe that I'm doing something wrong in my configuration that I'm just not noticing but I figured at the very least that `$map` variable should always be set.

**Edit:** The following will also produce the error but will return `true` for that validate call.

``` php
public function addRack($Rack, array $options = []) {

    $options += [
        'associated' => [
            'Devices.DeviceInformations'
        ],
        'atomic' => false
    ];
    $createRack = function () use ($Rack, $options) {
        $validates = $this->validate($Rack, $options);
        debug($validates);
        return false;
    };
    return $this->connection()->transactional($createRack);

}
```
